  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1249 PM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nAnother warm and dry October day is on display with clear skies\nand temperatures spanning the 80s as a 586dam ridge continues to\ndominate the region. Above normal temperatures and dry conditions\nwill remain the weather story here in the Mid-South through\ntomorrow with highs in the mid 80s to lower 90s. Come late\ntomorrow evening and into early Saturday, eyes turn to remnants\nof Hurricane Isaias. After making landfall along the Gulf Coast\nstates, it will begin to take a northern/northwestern trek,\nimpacting northeastern Mississippi early Saturday.\n\nThe primary impacts with Isaias' remnants will be gusty winds and\nperiods of heavy rainfall. Forecast PWATs begin to surge to\naround 1.7\", nearing the 95th percentile for this time of year,\nby early Saturday. One thing to note, much of the Mid-South is\ncurrently under severe (D2) to extreme (D3) drought with very dry\nsoils as little to no precipitation has fallen in quite some\ntime. With forecast amounts ranging between 1\" and 3\", with\nlocally higher amounts up to 4\", a Flood Watch has been issued\nfor Saturday through sunrise Sunday for most of our counties east\nof the Mississippi River. Latest NBM probabilities have a 30-50%\nchance of rainfall amounts through Sunday exceeding 3\" over\nnorthern Mississippi. Some models, such as the RRFS, have an\noverall dry solution with us here in the Mid-South being dry\nslotted on the west side of Isaias' remnants.\n\nAs far as winds go, we're expecting sustained winds up to 25 mph\nwith gusts up to 40 mph associated with this tropical system. A\nWind Advisory will likely be warranted for some portions of the\nMid-South Saturday, but will be issued in subsequent forecast\npackages. The exact evolution, intensity, and track of Hurricane\nIsaias remains uncertain at this time due to a few surface fronts\n(one currently extending across the Ohio River Valley and the\nother extending from central Florida through the northern Gulf)\nand the upper-level flow pattern. What we do know at this time\nis, Saturday will be wet and windy. As things currently stand, we\nare not expecting severe weather here in the Mid-South with\nIsaias' current track. If its remnants meander further west,\nfurther into our area, brief spin up tornadoes could become an\nissue. In any case, tornado potential remains almost non-\nmentionable at this time and if tornadoes do come into the story\nSaturday, they will be short-lived and best chances will exist\neast of the Mississippi River.\n\nIsaias will move fairly quickly across our area by noon Sunday.\nRain chances will diminish leaving a relatively pleasant and\noverall dry day with highs in the 70s and lower 80s. Monday\nthrough the remainder of the 7 day period, we're looking dry and\nwarm as upper-level ridging looks to broaden over the Lower\nMississippi Valley by mid-week. Above normal temperatures head\nback into the forecast beginning Monday with highs in the 80s to\nlower 90s once again.\n\nAEH",
